Below is a compact “road‑map” that shows  

1. which columns matter for every sub‑step of the problem and why,  
2. ten progressively more complex Snowflake queries (all limited to 20 rows) that can be run one after another to explore the data and ultimately retrieve the two required multipolygons.  

All identifiers are wrapped in double‑quotes, all comments are introduced with “--”.  

============================================================  
Column reference cheat‑sheet  

• GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.PLANET_FEATURES  
  • "feature_type": only keep ‘multipolygons’.  
  • "geometry" (BINARY): convert with ST_GEOGFROMWKB() to a GEOGRAPHY object.  
  • "all_tags" (VARIANT stored as text): contains keys like “wikidata”, “name”, etc.  
  • "osm_id": unique identifier of the multipolygon.  

• GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.PLANET_FEATURES_POINTS  
  • "geometry": point geometry to test containment in a multipolygon.  
  • "all_tags": may include the “name” or other useful attributes to inspect.  

============================================================  
10 step‑by‑step SQL snippets  

```sql
-- 1️⃣  Quick glance at how “all_tags” looks for multipolygons
SELECT "osm_id",
       SUBSTR("all_tags", 1, 300) AS "sample_tags"
FROM   GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.PLANET_FEATURES
WHERE  "feature_type" = 'multipolygons'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 2️⃣  Find the multipolygon that carries the Wikidata item Q191
SELECT "osm_id",
       ST_GEOGFROMWKB("geometry")      AS "geo",
       "all_tags"
FROM   GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.PLANET_FEATURES t,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => PARSE_JSON(t."all_tags")) f
WHERE  f.value:"key"  = 'wikidata'
  AND  f.value:"value" = 'Q191'
  AND  "feature_type" = 'multipolygons'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 3️⃣  Store that geometry into a CTE for re‑use
WITH q191 AS (
  SELECT ST_GEOGFROMWKB("geometry") AS geom
  FROM   GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.PLANET_FEATURES t,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => PARSE_JSON(t."all_tags")) f
  WHERE  f.value:"key"  = 'wikidata'
    AND  f.value:"value" = 'Q191'
    AND  "feature_type" = 'multipolygons'
  LIMIT 1
)
SELECT * FROM q191;
```

```sql
-- 4️⃣  List other multipolygons that spatially intersect the Q191 polygon
WITH q191 AS ( …same as above… )
SELECT p."osm_id",
       ST_AREA(ST_GEOGFROMWKB(p."geometry")) AS "area_m2",
       p."all_tags"
FROM   GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.PLANET_FEATURES p, q191
WHERE  p."feature_type" = 'multipolygons'
  AND  ST_INTERSECTS(q191.geom, ST_GEOGFROMWKB(p."geometry"))
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 5️⃣  Filter out those that already have ANY wikidata tag themselves
WITH q191 AS ( … ),
     others AS (
       SELECT p.*
       FROM   GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.PLANET_FEATURES p, q191
       WHERE  p."feature_type" = 'multipolygons'
         AND  ST_INTERSECTS(q191.geom, ST_GEOGFROMWKB(p."geometry"))
     )
SELECT o."osm_id", o."all_tags"
FROM   others o
WHERE  NOT EXISTS (
        SELECT 1
        FROM   LATERAL FLATTEN(input => PARSE_JSON(o."all_tags")) f
        WHERE  f.value:"key" = 'wikidata'
      )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6️⃣  Extract the “name” tag for those candidate multipolygons
WITH q191 AS ( … ),
     candidates AS ( …same query as step 5… )
SELECT c."osm_id",
       ( SELECT f.value:"value"
         FROM   LATERAL FLATTEN(input => PARSE_JSON(c."all_tags")) f
         WHERE  f.value:"key" = 'name'
         LIMIT 1
       ) AS "name"
FROM   candidates c
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7️⃣  Bring in all point features first, restricting to Q191’s envelope for speed
WITH q191 AS ( … )
SELECT p.*
FROM   GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.PLANET_FEATURES_POINTS p, q191
WHERE  ST_DWITHIN(q191.geom, ST_GEOGFROMWKB(p."geometry"), 1000000) -- 1,000 km rough window
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8️⃣  Count how many points fall INSIDE each candidate multipolygon
WITH q191 AS ( … ),
     candidates AS ( …step 5… ),
     points AS (
       SELECT ST_GEOGFROMWKB("geometry") AS geo_pt
       FROM   GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.PLANET_FEATURES_POINTS
     )
SELECT  c."osm_id",
        ( SELECT f.value:"value"
          FROM   LATERAL FLATTEN(input => PARSE_JSON(c."all_tags")) f
          WHERE  f.value:"key"='name' LIMIT 1 ) AS "name",
        COUNT(*) AS pts_inside
FROM    candidates c
JOIN    points    p
ON      ST_CONTAINS(ST_GEOGFROMWKB(c."geometry"), p.geo_pt)
GROUP BY 1,2
ORDER BY pts_inside DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9️⃣  Rank candidates and keep only the top two by point count
WITH ranked AS ( …same as step 8… )
SELECT *
FROM   ranked
QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY pts_inside DESC) <= 2;
```

```sql
-- 🔟  Final query that produces only name and point count for the biggest two
WITH q191 AS ( … ),
     candidates AS ( … ),
     points AS ( … )
SELECT  ( SELECT f.value:"value"
          FROM   LATERAL FLATTEN(input => PARSE_JSON(c."all_tags")) f
          WHERE  f.value:"key"='name' LIMIT 1 ) AS "multipolygon_name",
        COUNT(*)                                     AS "points_inside"
FROM    candidates c
JOIN    points    p
ON      ST_CONTAINS(ST_GEOGFROMWKB(c."geometry"), p.geo_pt)
GROUP BY 1
ORDER BY 2 DESC
LIMIT 2;
```

Run the queries in sequence (substituting each “…same as above…” CTE with the exact text from earlier snippets) and the last statement (#10) will list the two non‑wikidata multipolygons inside the Q191 area that contain the most points, together with their names.
